  "semantic_findings": [
    {
      "topic": "Source convention",
      "result": "PASS with explicit conventional-scope limitation",
      "detail": "Both languages name (z;q)_N without a product definition. DLMF 17.2.1 independently verifies the standard finite q-Pochhammer product over k=0,...,N-1 of (1-z q^k). The informal label partial q-binomial does not specify a different series, coefficient sequence, scaling, or truncation. The formal polynomial directly implements the standard displayed symbol. Unknown author intention cannot be certified; an authoritative nonstandard redefinition would require a new review.",
      "primary_sources": [
        "https://dlmf.nist.gov/17.2.E1",
        "https://dlmf.nist.gov/5.18.E1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Bilingual scope",
      "result": "PASS",
      "detail": "English and Chinese each assert the same explicit relative asymptotic formula for real 0<q<1. Chinese explicitly marks it asymptotic. N tends to infinity through positive integers with q fixed; no coupled q(N) limit is specified."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Actual roots and multiplicity",
      "result": "PASS",
      "detail": "qPochhammer is an actual Complex polynomial built with standard polynomial operations; qPochhammer_eval matches the standard product. Each factor is nonzero (evaluation at 0 is 1), so the product is nonzero. For q nonzero each factor has exactly the singleton root ((q:Complex)^k)^(-1). Polynomial.roots_prod produces a multiset union, not a set that drops multiplicity. qPochhammer_roots proves equality with the complete actual root multiset, and qPochhammer_roots_card proves its cardinality is N. For 0<q<1 roots are distinct as a mathematical observation, but no simplicity assumption is needed by the proof."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Modulus and geometric mean",
      "result": "PASS",
      "detail": "rootModulusProduct applies Complex.instNorm to every entry of Polynomial.roots and multiplies the multiset. Compiled pp.all output confirms standard complex norm and standard Real power instances. The geometric mean uses Real.rpow with real exponent 1/(N:Real). Nonnegativity and geometricMean_pow prove the nonnegative Nth-root properties for positive N. N=0 is an irrelevant total-function extension because the eventual threshold is >=1."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Exact calculation",
      "result": "PASS",
      "detail": "For q>0, product of actual root moduli equals product_{k<N}(q^k)^(-1)=q^(-N(N-1)/2). The actual geometric mean for N>=1 is therefore q^(-(N-1)/2). sum_range_real supplies the exact finite sum. All negative exponents, 1/N, and N/2 in the analytic statements use Real arithmetic after coercion, so there is no natural subtraction or integer rounding error."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Relative Big-O and quantifiers",
      "result": "PASS",
      "detail": "claimedMainTerm preserves the factor 1/(1+q). relativeError is g/A-1, correctly putting the error inside the multiplicative parenthesis. RelativeAsymptotic says there exists a positive real C and positive integer threshold N0 such that all N>=N0 obey |relativeError|<=C*errorScale. These may depend on fixed q, exactly ordinary pointwise Big-O. relativeAsymptotic_iff_isBigO proves equivalence with Mathlib Asymptotics.IsBigO atTop, using standard real norms. No uniformity in q or restriction to a subsequence is imposed."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Asymptotic contradiction",
      "result": "PASS",
      "detail": "For every admissible q and N>=1 the relative error is exactly q>0. errorScale=q^((N:Real)/2) tends to zero because it is (q^(1/2))^N with base in [0,1). A presumed eventual bound C*errorScale is eventually strictly less than q and contradicts that bound. The universal NecessaryClause is then falsified at q=1/2 (which is admissible and algebraic); no finite-N violation is incorrectly substituted for asymptotic failure."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Logical sufficiency",
      "result": "PASS with explicit scope",
      "detail": "The explicit formula is a necessary conjunct of every completion of the source that retains the standard polynomial and displayed asymptotic. Its negation suffices to disprove those completions. no_completion states the correct logical implication (FullSource -> NecessaryClause) -> not FullSource. Neither the Lean theorem nor this review equates NecessaryClause with the entire source or claims an independent formalization of the unspecified theta/Jensen/transcendence clauses. A positive proof of NecessaryClause alone would not suffice, but this is its negation."
    }
  ],
